{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-84841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/assets/46929b27eb98506479401e2b78ea18fac539a11dcda4ecfb7b90c700dec61fdb.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-84841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(1,-3),$ and passes through the point $(2,-4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(1,-3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-1=p{t}^{2},\\,y+3=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(2,-4),$ we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=-4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -1=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ 1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{4p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=(\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y+3=2(\\frac{1}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+3=\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-84841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb67ee7103b14ebe343eb7adab4f94f0aed04e66bb41f728230e171115f90223"} {"question" "q-84841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-84841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+1=2{t}^{2},\\,y-3=4t"] ["math" "x+1=4{t}^{2},\\,y-3=8t"] ["math" "x-1=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+3=\\frac{1}{2}t"] ["math" "x-1=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+3=\\frac{1}{2}t"] ["math" "x-1=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+3=\\frac{1}{8}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-84841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-1=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+3=\\frac{1}{2}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/assets/6cd9c6a97d450761c01a9adb723985acbf4e5dc91e9877d7bd46c95db66d8c30.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(3,1),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-7).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y-1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,-7),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-7$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -8=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =2pt \\\\ -4 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(-\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -8 & =\\frac{16}{p} \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-2){t}^{2},\\,y-1=2(-2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-4t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb67ee7103b14ebe343eb7adab4f94f0aed04e66bb41f728230e171115f90223"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-4t"] ["math" "x+3=-{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-2t"] ["math" "x-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-4t"] ["math" "x-3=-4t,\\,\\,y-1=-2{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-3=-{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-2t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-4t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-5)^{2}=2(x-2)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(2,5).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-5=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =2(x-2) \\\\ 4p(x-2) & =2(x-2) \\\\ 4p & =2 \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y-5=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-5=t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb67ee7103b14ebe343eb7adab4f94f0aed04e66bb41f728230e171115f90223"} {"question" "q-106464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+5=t"] ["math" "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=2t"] ["math" "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=t"] ["math" "x-5=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-2=t"] ["math" "x-5=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-2=-4t,\\,y-5=-2{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x-2}{4},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x-2}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-5}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{y-5}{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-8(y-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb67ee7103b14ebe343eb7adab4f94f0aed04e66bb41f728230e171115f90223"} {"question" "q-106502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x-2)^{2}=-8(y-5)"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}=8(y-5)"] ["math" "(x-5)^{2}=-8(y-2)"] ["math" "(y-5)^{2}=-8(x-2)"] ["math" "(y-5)^{2}=8(x-2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-2)^{2}=-8(y-5)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+3=-3{t}^{2},\\,y+5=-6t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x+3}{3},\\,t=-\\frac{y+5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+5}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{x+3}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{36} & =-\\frac{x+3}{3} \\\\ (y+5)^{2} & =36\\cdot (-\\frac{x+3}{3}) \\\\ (y+5)^{2} & =-12(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106505" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cb67ee7103b14ebe343eb7adab4f94f0aed04e66bb41f728230e171115f90223"} {"question" "q-106505" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106505" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{(x+3)}^{2}=-12(y+5)"] ["math" "{(y+3)}^{2}=-12(x+5)"] ["math" "{(y+3)}^{2}=12(x+5)"] ["math" "{(y+5)}^{2}=-12(x+3)"] ["math" "{(y+5)}^{2}=12(x+3)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"} {"question" "q-106505" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(y+5)}^{2}=-12(x+3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "16b771c8d0a368dcfa43460867108efac233374ab79b877c5e4ed3b2b021ddfe"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1187 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
